From 1ccc2bd1ceb52415a8ac8e806a58000f0121d002 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rehanraza786 <rehanraza786@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 23:54:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/12] Make insights durable: docs/insights.json as source of
 truth
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Insights were the only memory layer without a file-backed source of
truth. ADRs live in docs/adr/, assertions in docs/graph-assertions.json,
both re-derived on every index. Insights existed only in .ariadne/index.db,
which is gitignored — so pull-index.sh (mv over index.db) and the corruption
recovery path (moves the DB aside, rebuilds fresh) destroyed every
save_insight and every graph-view note silently. --rebuild happened to spare
them, which made the loss intermittent rather than obvious. This is the same
failure save_decision already guards against for ADRs.

- indexer owns the insights DDL and re-loads docs/insights.json on full and
  incremental index, from the workspace parent AND every root, so a file
  placed either way loads; only --rebuild wipes the table, so the file is
  exactly authoritative there and a normal index stays additive
- save_insight and annotate (the VS Code graph view's write path) write the
  file first, then the row; both prefer a git-versioned root, annotate
  inferring it from the repo-prefixed target and falling back to the
  workspace parent rather than demanding a new argument
- enrich exports docs/insights.json alongside the gitignored
  docs/generated/insights.md report
- save_decision's multi-root anchoring is extracted and shared

Four related defects in the same layer:

- module insights never went stale: explain gated the hash check on
  kind == "file", so a module insight was served forever while the tool
  description promised it auto-staled
- save_insight and annotate hashed module files ordered by path while enrich
  sorts by hash, so the three never agreed and enrich regenerated and
  overwrote every hand-written module insight on each run
- explain used `target=? OR target LIKE ?` with no ORDER BY, so a substring
  match could outrank the exact row
- topic/table notes, which annotate has always accepted, had no ingest path;
  naively normalizing them to "module" would make explain compute a module
  hash for a topic

Node and Python editions at parity. run_tests.py gains four assertions: an
insight survives --rebuild, an annotate note survives it with its kind
intact, the file it writes is inside a git-versioned repo, and the enrich
and server module hashes agree.
